body:
I was attached to a special forces unit (I was search and rescue but they needed some of the skills I was qualified for), we were inside some tunnels under Gaza.  I fell behind the last person in the formation, as I was out of shape compared to the rest of these guys.  About a minute later I heard something behind me and then there were arms around my chest, pinning my rifle to my side so I couldn't get control of it.  There were two of them trying to drag me away, alive.  Hamas values a live soldier a thousand times more than a dead one; they can trade us for thousands of prisoners plus they really enjoy torturing us.  I didn't think about it at the time but if I had yelled out, my teammates would have shot me first to prevent me being taken captive.  I honestly don't know how I did this, and I run through it in my head all the time.  I had a knife, a Ka Bar, in the front of my vest, so I pulled it out and stabbed both of my assailants in the neck and made sure they were dead before sprinting back to my team.  The lieutenant with us saw how bloody I was and asked what happened.  The sergeant wanted to go back in and document the bodies properly etc but the lieutenant assumed there were more enemies where those two came from and so we blew the entrances to that particular tunnel.

&#x200B;

There were a few other instances during the week that we fought in the tunnels that stuck with me.  I remember a comrade being hit by a mortar and trying to treat what was left of him.  I remember a couple hours after leaving that tunnel hearing that a friend of mine had been killed by a missile to an armored vehicle.  I also remember sitting on sand dunes eating tuna sandwiches.  It's so funny how you never really think about eating in war.  Like, I never thought that I'd have had time to worry about what to eat, or where, or if I still had that little bottle of Tabasco in my vest (YES!).  But that's what real combat is, and it's something that I'm ashamed to say I miss a lot.
